+# BLOCKED: 002_unique_email cannot be rolled out to production yet
+
+## What is required
+
+`users.email` must become:
+
+- required (`NOT NULL`), and
+- unique ignoring case (`UNIQUE` on `lower(email)`).
+
+The migration must not modify or delete any customer rows; Support owns that data.
+
+## Why this is impossible against the current production snapshot
+
+`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql` (anonymised production snapshot, 2026-10-01) already
+violates both parts of the requested invariant:
+
+| Problem | Rows | Detail |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| NULL email | `id = 17` (Goran Okafor) | `email IS NULL`, so `NOT NULL` cannot be added. |
+| Case-insensitive duplicate | `id = 1`, `id = 4`, `id = 23` | `ana.silva@example.com`, `Ana.Silva@example.com`, `ana.silva@example.com` all normalise to `ana.silva@example.com`. A unique index on `lower(email)` cannot be built. |
+
+`id = 23` is also an exact duplicate of `id = 1`, in addition to the
+case-insensitive collision with `id = 4`.
+
+SQLite enforces these constraints when the index is built and when the table is
+rebuilt. There is no way to install a genuine `NOT NULL` constraint or a genuine
+unique index over data that already violates it without either:
+
+- updating/deleting the offending customer rows, which this task forbids, or
+- skipping the offending rows (partial index / triggers / disabling checks),
+  which would leave production data violating the invariant and silently
+  pretend the migration succeeded.
+
+I did not do either. A workaround that leaves bad rows in place would make the
+schema claim an invariant the data does not satisfy.
+
+## What is done
+
+- `migrations/002_unique_email.sql` contains the intended change: rebuild
+  `users` with `email TEXT NOT NULL`, then add
+  `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_lower_unique ON users (lower(email))`.
+- It has a preflight guard that aborts with a clear
+  `002_unique_email blocked: ...` message **before any DDL** if any existing row
+  is NULL or case-insensitively duplicated. It does not touch customer rows.
+- On a fresh/clean database it applies normally, and the resulting schema
+  rejects NULL emails and case-insensitive duplicates.
+- `fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql` was not edited.
+
+## What Support must decide/do before this migration can run
+
+1. Give `id = 17` a real email (or decide how a missing email should be
+   represented). `NOT NULL` requires a value.
+2. Resolve the `ana.silva@example.com` collision among `id = 1`, `id = 4`, and
+   `id = 23`: choose the canonical row and reassign or remove the others. This
+   is a data-ownership decision, not something the migration may guess.
+3. Optionally re-check for other violations with:
+
+   ```sql
+   SELECT id, email
+   FROM users
+   WHERE email IS NULL
+      OR lower(email) IN (
+          SELECT lower(email) FROM users
+          WHERE email IS NOT NULL
+          GROUP BY lower(email) HAVING count(*) > 1
+      )
+   ORDER BY id;
+   ```
+
+Once the snapshot is clean, `002_unique_email.sql` can be applied unchanged.
+
+## Test status
+
+- `test_constraint_on_fresh_db`: passes (clean DB gets the constraints).
+- `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot`: blocked by the preflight above. The
+  migration intentionally aborts instead of changing/deleting rows or installing
+  a constraint that the data violates.
